      "text": "To find the area of the square, we need to first determine the length of one side of the square. Since the square and the triangle have equal perimeters, we can start by calculating the perimeter of the triangle.\n\nThe perimeter of the triangle is the sum of the lengths of its three sides:\n\\[ \\text{Perimeter of the triangle} = 6.1 \\, \\text{cm} + 8.2 \\, \\text{cm} + 9.7 \\, \\text{cm} = 24 \\, \\text{cm} \\]\n\nSince the square has the same perimeter as the triangle, the perimeter of the square is also 24 cm. The perimeter of a square is given by 4 times the length of one side. Let's denote the side length of the square by \\( s \\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[ 4s = 24 \\]\nSolving for \\( s \\), we get:\n\\[ s = \\frac{24}{4} = 6 \\, \\text{cm} \\]\n\nNow that we know the side length of the square is 6 cm, we can calculate the area of the square. The area \\( A \\) of a square is given by the formula:\n\\[ A = s^2 \\]\nSubstituting the value of \\( s \\):\n\\[ A = 6^2 = 36 \\, \\text{cm}^2 \\]\n\nThus, the area of the square is:\n\\[ \\boxed{36} \\]",
